        "question": "How did the topography of the Deccan influence the geopolitical strategy of the Satavahana Empire compared to the Magadhan empires of the North? A) The flat, unhindered plains of the Deccan allowed for a massive centralized bureaucracy identical to the Mauryas. B) The rugged terrain, divided by the Western Ghats and major river valleys, forced the Satavahanas to adopt a decentralized, semi-feudal political network, relying on feudatories like the Maharathis and Mahabhojas who held immense regional autonomy. C) The complete lack of rivers forced them to rely purely on naval power in the Arabian Sea. D) They built a continuous wall across the Vindhyas to isolate themselves from the North. Ans: B (Explanation: The Deccan's geography prevented absolute centralization; hence, they integrated local chieftains (Maharathis) by giving them high status and matrimonial ties to hold the empire together.) Q69. The flourishing of Mahayana Buddhism under the Satavahanas (and later Ikshvakus) in the Krishna basin (Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda) is sociologically linked to:",
